    text: `At noon in July, the shortest route across our central plaza is also the route least often taken. People trace the thin shadow of the courthouse, wait beneath the awning of a closed shop, or cross the street twice to remain under the sycamores. We usually describe these choices as matters of comfort. They are also evidence about access.

A bench in full sun is not equally available to every resident. The delivery driver between shifts, the parent with an infant, and the older neighbor managing a heart condition experience the same hundred degrees differently. A city that counts benches but ignores whether people can safely use them has measured furniture, not service.

Some object that trees are decorative expenses and that fabric canopies deteriorate. Both claims contain a warning worth hearing: plantings require water and care, while poorly chosen structures become costly debris. That is an argument for maintenance plans, not for bare pavement. We do not reject streetlights because bulbs fail. We budget for replacement because illumination makes public space usable after dark.

Shade deserves the same practical treatment. A mature tree cools the air through evapotranspiration, intercepts storm water, and reduces the heat absorbed by concrete. Where soil conditions make trees impractical, a repaired arcade or a solar canopy can serve. The right solution will vary block by block; the obligation to provide one should not.

Next month the council will vote on the plaza renovation. Renderings show handsome stone, a fountain, and rows of new seats. Yet the plan places most of those seats beyond the afternoon shadow line. Before approving an image of public life, the council should test the place where that life is supposed to occur. Walk the plaza at noon. Measure surface temperatures. Ask who stays and who hurries through. Then move the seats, preserve the trees, and fund their care. A public square should invite the public to remain.`,
