    text: `Dear Committee Members,

You have asked whether the county's new online map should display only information that has been checked by a surveyor. Accuracy is a worthy ambition, but the proposed rule confuses uncertainty with error.

Consider the footpaths crossing the northern farms. Some are recorded in deeds; others have been used openly for generations but were never measured. If the map omits the latter until a survey is complete, a clean screen will give a false impression: not “the route is uncertain,” but “no route exists.” Blankness is not neutral when readers reasonably treat the map as a picture of the county.

The alternative is not to publish rumor as fact. The map can distinguish kinds of knowledge. A solid line may mark a surveyed public road, a dashed line a documented customary path, and a shaded band a route whose precise location remains disputed. Each feature can link to its evidence and revision date. Such symbols admit what we know and what we do not.

This approach will make the map look less finished. Good. A public record should not borrow authority from polish it has not earned. The old paper maps contained confident boundaries that later surveys moved by hundreds of feet. Their neat ink did not make them true; it merely made correction embarrassing.

Nor will visible uncertainty weaken public trust. Trust is not the expectation that an institution never revises itself. It is the expectation that the institution will show its work, correct mistakes, and distinguish strong evidence from weak. Meteorologists do this when they publish forecast ranges. Scientists do it when they report margins of error. County government can do it when drawing a footpath.

I therefore recommend that the committee adopt a layered map with a plainly worded legend, a date on every record, and a process through which residents may submit evidence for review. The map will become more accurate not by waiting in silence for perfect knowledge, but by making revision part of its design.

Respectfully,
The County Archivist`,
