  addSet({
    id: "r-observers",
    type: "reading",
    title: "The Discipline of Noticing",
    text: `When our watershed project invited residents to report the first spring appearance of three frog species, I expected enthusiasm and noisy data. We received both. One observer entered a tree frog in January because a porch hinge squeaked. Another reported twelve frogs but no location. Critics cited such records as proof that public observation cannot contribute to science.

They mistook raw submissions for finished evidence. Professional field notes also contain errors; what makes them useful is a system designed to detect and describe those errors. We built that system in public. The reporting form began requiring coordinates and a short audio clip. Experienced volunteers reviewed uncertain calls without seeing the observer's name. Records were marked in three categories: confirmed, probable, or unresolved, rather than forced into yes or no.

The revisions improved the dataset, but their greater effect was on the observers. People who once wrote “frog near pond” began noting temperature, time, recent rainfall, and the interval between calls. They learned that noticing is not a private flash of recognition. It is a claim another person should be able to examine.

This does not make every volunteer a biologist, nor should it erase the distinction between a weekend observation and a controlled survey. Our reports cluster near roads and homes; they say more about places people visit than places frogs inhabit. We publish a map of that sampling bias beside the species map. Used alone, the records cannot estimate total population. Combined with systematic surveys, however, they can identify unusual dates and locations worth investigating.

Last March, three independent observers uploaded clips of a species calling two weeks earlier than in our previous records. The reports did not prove a climate trend. Because we preserved uncertain reports, professionals could compare locations and plan a targeted survey, which confirmed frogs at two of the sites. The public observations served science not by replacing expertise, but by directing attention.

Citizen science is sometimes praised as a way to collect free labor. That description is too small. Its best product is a public practiced in making claims proportionate to evidence—and institutions practiced in showing how evidence becomes knowledge.`,
  }, [
    ["1.A", "The writer's primary purpose is to", "show how structured public observations can support expert research", "argue that volunteers should conduct all future watershed surveys", "demonstrate that frog calls are easier to identify than visual observations", "prove that early frog activity is caused by climate change", "The passage emphasizes review, uncertainty labels, bias disclosure, and targeted professional surveys, presenting citizen reports as a complement to—not a substitute for—expertise."],
    ["1.B", "The opening admission of erroneous reports primarily helps the writer address readers who", "doubt the reliability of data submitted by nonprofessionals", "oppose studying animals near roads and homes", "already volunteer regularly for the watershed project", "believe that professional field notes contain no observations", "By presenting the porch-hinge and location errors before critics do, the writer confronts the strongest credibility objection and then explains how the project redesigned verification."],
    ["1.A", "The writer's reference to “our watershed project” establishes a perspective based on", "direct involvement in the reporting process", "complete neutrality toward whether citizen science succeeds", "legal responsibility for regulating every watershed resident", "personal expertise in long-range climate modeling", "First-person plural details show operational involvement with the form, reviewers, maps, and surveys. The passage does not claim regulatory or climate-modeling authority."],
    ["3.A", "Which evidence most clearly supports the claim that volunteers learned more disciplined observation?", "Reports began including temperature, time, rainfall, and intervals between calls.", "One observer initially mistook a squeaking hinge for a frog.", "The project invited reports about three frog species.", "Professional field notes can also contain occasional errors.", "The added contextual details are a direct change in participants' practice and make their claims more examinable, unlike the initial mistake or general comparison."],
    ["3.B", "The passage's thesis is best expressed as which statement?", "Verified, qualified citizen observations can guide expert inquiry.", "Large numbers of volunteer reports can eliminate sampling bias from ecological studies.", "Audio recordings are sufficient to estimate the total population of a species.", "Public participation is useful mainly because it reduces the cost of professional labor.", "The writer's positive case depends on systems of review, honest limits, and follow-up. The conclusion explicitly rejects “free labor” as an adequate account of the value."],
    ["3.C", "The writer qualifies the value of the project by noting that the reports", "cannot independently estimate total frog populations", "were all collected before the reporting form required locations", "contain more precise information than controlled surveys", "can demonstrate a climate trend from a single unusual spring", "The limits paragraph identifies spatial sampling bias and an inference the records cannot support, preventing the argument from treating abundant submissions as representative population data."],
    ["5.A", "The early-spring reports in the fifth paragraph function as", "a case where public reports prompt tests without proving a trend", "statistical proof that frog populations are increasing throughout the watershed", "evidence that the project's uncertainty categories were unnecessary", "an example of professional surveys being less accurate than volunteer reports", "The writer carefully separates prompting a survey from proving a climate trend, illustrating the proportional claim-making advocated throughout the passage."],
    ["5.B", "The second and third paragraphs are connected chiefly through a movement from", "changes in verification procedures to changes in observers' habits", "one frog species to a complete catalogue of watershed animals", "professional criticism to a rejection of public participation", "anecdotal evidence to a population estimate", "The second paragraph describes required coordinates, clips, blind review, and labels; the third shows participants responding by making more detailed, examinable observations."],
    ["5.C", "The writer's use of “confirmed, probable, or unresolved” chiefly provides", "classification that preserves degrees of evidentiary confidence", "a narrative sequence describing a frog's life cycle", "a comparison between three different watersheds", "a definition that makes all records equally reliable", "The categories prevent uncertain submissions from being forced into a binary decision and model how the project communicates varying confidence."],
    ["7.A", "The phrase “noisy data” in the first sentence refers both to frog reports and to", "information containing errors and irrelevant variation", "the complete absence of recorded sound", "residents objecting loudly to the project", "equipment that amplifies every audio clip", "The phrase plays on literal frog sound and the statistical sense of noise as unreliable variation, efficiently anticipating the project's central challenge."],
    ["7.B", "In the sentence beginning “Because we preserved uncertain reports,” the dependent clause chiefly", "identifies the project practice that made later follow-up possible", "concedes that uncertain reports should have been removed immediately", "presents the later survey as a cause of the original volunteer reports", "introduces a reason that contradicts the passage's defense of transparency", "The because-clause names preserved uncertainty as the reason professionals could identify a location for later investigation, linking review practice to a concrete scientific use."],
    ["7.C", "The colon before the list “confirmed, probable, or unresolved” chiefly", "introduces the categories used to explain the review status of reports", "marks the three terms as a quotation from a critic of the project", "shows that the categories are interchangeable descriptions of certainty", "separates the central claim from an unrelated conclusion about volunteers", "The colon tells readers that the three labels specify the review categories. That punctuation supports the passage's emphasis on making evidentiary status explicit."],
  ]);

